# Colours come apart: a dot of ink on paper, water climbing past it, and the colours the ink was mixed
# from left standing at different heights, so two pens that look the same on the page do not look the
# same on the strip.
lesson chemistry-colours-come-apart v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=4 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Colours come apart"
  goal "Read a paper strip where an ink has come apart, and say which pen drew a dot from the colours it left."

  look {
    scene 15x16 {
      chromatography strips inks=["black1", "black2", "black3"] labels=["A", "B", "C"] run=1 beaker=1 ruler=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
    }
    say """
      Most inks are mixtures. Draw a dot near the foot of a paper strip, stand the strip in a little
      water, and the water climbs the paper and carries the ink up with it. The colours in the mixture
      do not travel at the same speed, so they end up as separate bands at different heights, and the
      black dot turns out to have been yellow, pink and blue all along. Two black pens can leave
      different bands, which is how the strips tell them apart.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice chemistry.ink-how-many count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked chemistry.ink-furthest n=2 level=medium
    }
    practice chemistry.ink-furthest count=2 seed=4 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chemistry.ink-which-pen count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chemistry.ink-same-pen count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level hard {
      show chemistry.ink-two-alike p=3
    }
  }
  story {
    show chemistry.saltflats-ink v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=3
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chemistry.ink-two-alike p=1 easy-p=0 hard-p=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chemistry.ink-which-wrote v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "An ink is often a mixture, and water climbing paper leaves its colours in bands at different heights."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea here is that a mixture can be taken apart without changing what it is made of, and that
    the parts can be recognised afterwards. The strips do that in front of a child: the same black dot
    leaves different bands depending on the pen, which is why the method is used to tell inks apart in
    earnest. Watch for a child who counts the dot on the pencil line as one of the colours, and for one
    who reads a band's colour but not its height; the two-star question turns on height, since two pens
    can share a colour and leave it in a different place. The three-star question gives a clue in two
    halves and four strips, and a child who uses only the first half will be left with two. Off the
    page, a strip of kitchen paper, a felt pen, and a saucer with a centimetre of water, which works in
    about ten minutes and is worth doing with a black pen and a brown one side by side.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the ink to count comes apart into two colours, a strip's highest band is read once in the teacher's pen before two more on the shorter strips, the pen to name is one of the two-colour pens, the pair to judge is plainly two pens, the story counts four bands over two strips, the two-star pair shares its pink band, and the three-star clue rules out three strips on the first half alone."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the ink to count comes apart into three colours, the strips read on the ruler are the tallest two, the pen to name is a black one, the pair to judge shares a band at the same height, the core carries a second pair to tell apart, the story counts six bands over two strips, the two-star pair shares its blue band at seven, and the three-star clue needs both halves."
  }
}
